A: Unfortunately, Pex framework does not have the capability to analyze contracts that deal with multiple collections like your tests. It only performs equivalence verification for methods that take a single argument of any type. You can specify multiple sets of input parameters using PexSource and PexAllValues attributes, but only on methods that have a single parameter.
One possible workaround is to create separate methods for each operation (Add, Remove, etc.) and call these from your test method. Since Pex verifies each method separately and separately, this will ensure that you have contracts for each operation. However, be aware that a combination of all these operations will result in a highly complex Pex test method and could significantly slow down your Pex testing.
So in a nutshell, your options are to divide it up or use a different tool for contract verification - something that can handle multiple collections and parameter types.
Hope this helps.

A: It seems like the contract checking part of Pex is not able to test contracts on methods that deal with multiple collections or parameters. A workaround could be creating separate methods for each operation, and then call these from your test method. Keep in mind, this will produce complex and slow tests.
As for other options, you might need to consider an alternative tool for contracts check. For example, ReSharper would handle contracts in a great way, and NUnit contracts--------------------	            Debug.Assert(() => (New_Ret >= 0) == (New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0));
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is non-negative, last index of x in new array is >= 0 
	            Debug.Assert(() => (New_Ret >= 0) == (New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0));
        }
    }
