//Assuming the tests have been performed with the default parameters, the code does work correctly and the only way to ensure 
//that the code is “correct” is if the Pex engine finds a series of inputs that falsify the contract(s) (i.e., the output or
//side-effects do not behave as expected under the input conditions).
//An assertion is being checked to insure that the index of x in new array always gives the index in old array.
//And to insure that the last index of x in new array always given the last index in old array. The tests will help to ensure that this behaviour will always be true.
//Finally, the generated input cannot be a bug in the code, therefore it must not introduce a new bug into the system under test.
//The contract tests assume every method in this class returns the expected value and that side-effects as defined in the corresponding .NET
//metadata (e.g., Add() method will insert the specified object at the end of the ArrayList, and return the new count). If this is not the case, 
//this test will find some faulty inputs. Pex is smart enough to handle this and also the test cases will be designed by considering all possible corner cases. 

//Pex is also capable of handling exceptions for invalid inputs. It has the ability to generate test cases that exercise code that is currently failing. If a bug is found in the code and a new contract test 
//throws an exception then it means that the test case is a good coverage point and likely the original exception.

//Finally, Pex is not only capable of finding bugs, it can also generate a lot of test cases to increase the code coverage, to make the code more predictable i.e., when some particular input 
//comes it will not fail.
//For more detailed testing, you can check the coverage reports and other statistics given by Pex.

//Lastly, note that the contracts in this class only cover some of the methods provided by ArrayList class. To fully test this class you would need to write additional tests for other methods provided by the class.
//The purpose of these tests is to ensure that all methods behave as expected in theory and to test on some random values. If any method deviates from its contract then Pex would find 
//a good test case that has triggered the bug.
//If you have any questions, please feel free to ask.

//P.S. Please remember to install Pex on your machine and also install NUnit on your machine where it will be running the tests. Also please make sure that ArrayList.dll (your actual output assembly 
//which will be transformed by the Pex) is located in a directory listed in the path of your test cases in the NUnit tests. Also note that the contract must not only cover the theory that
//the inputs are invalid but also must cover the actual behaviors from the real object-oriented code.

//P.S.S. You should be aware that Pex isn't perfect and also have some limit to what it can find out. It's a tool to help you find bugs and not everything it might not find will need 
//to be fixed. The main focus of Pex is to assist in finding edge cases and potential exceptions in the code.

//P.S.S.S. I would prefer to note that if the tests are not passing properly or the test is taking a lot of time then it might not be a good test hence you may want to consider
//adding more constraints on the inputs for the better performance.
