A: The contracts that Microsoft Pex generates are very thorough, but sometimes they can be a bit too much. As you mentioned, you can ignore or disable certain contracts under Settings.

Unfortunately, this cannot be done directly from Visual Studio. This is a configuration issue and may be hard to reproduce. I would suggest creating a support request with Microsoft directly, explaining the issue and asking them to increase the number of ignored or disabled contracts.

Alternatively, you can write your own contracts if these default contracts don't meet your needs. This, of course, is more work and will require a deep understanding of the internals of the ArrayList class.

If you just want to add a few specific tests, then you can do that manually.

But be sure to only write the necessary tests, not too many (that may exceed your allocated time), because this task is time consuming. Tests that are not required, are not necessary. 

It could also be beneficial to raise this as a suggestion against some limitations in Pex testing. 

Let's hope Microsoft will consider this and fix it for future development or provide an easier way to ignore certain tests.

Remember, Pex is not perfect, and what we may consider as ideal can be in fact its limitation, in this case, the number of acceptable contracts.

However, it's still a very helpful framework to use for testing methods that might handle any arbitrary length collection or array.

I'd like to note too that Microsoft's Pex team is constantly adding enhancements to their testing tool. They are indeed continuously improving and that could include improvements to handle specific situations or collections. You should keep an eye--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret < New_arrListCount); 
        }

